  "preview": "A concert promoter surveys every 25th ticket-holder in line to predict snack sales for a 6,400-seat arena. Will the concessions stand sell out of nachos? And how much profit walks out the door?",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Use a random sample to make quantitative predictions about a population. Combine proportional reasoning with multi-step money calculations, and judge whether a sample is large enough or representative enough to trust.",
    "studentPrompt": "A concert promoter is planning concessions for a sold-out 6,400-seat arena. To predict snack sales, a worker surveys every 25th ticket-holder waiting in line at the main entrance, asking what one snack they plan to buy. The table shows the results from the 80 fans surveyed.\n\n| Snack          | Number who picked it | Price ($) | Cost to make ($) |\n|----------------|---------------------:|----------:|-----------------:|\n| Pretzel        | 24                   | 6.00      | 1.25             |\n| Nachos         | 18                   | 8.50      | 2.40             |\n| Popcorn        | 22                   | 5.50      | 0.80             |\n| Hot dog        | 12                   | 7.00      | 2.10             |\n| Nothing        | 4                    | —         | —                |\n\n1. Based on the sample, about how many of each snack should the promoter expect to sell?\n2. The nacho station can prep 1,600 orders before the show. Will the station run out? By about how much?\n3. Profit per item is (price − cost). What total profit should the promoter expect from concessions?\n4. The worker surveyed only fans at the MAIN entrance. Three other entrances were not sampled. Does that change how much you trust the prediction? What would you do differently?",
    "image": {
      "alt": "A side-on view of a concert arena: a stage with stage lights on the left, rows of seats filling the middle, and a concessions counter at the back with signs for pretzels, nachos, popcorn, and hot dogs.",
      "svg": "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 480 220\" font-family=\"system-ui, sans-serif\"><defs><linearGradient id=\"bg\" x1=\"0\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"1\"><stop offset=\"0\" stop-color=\"#1a1530\"/><stop offset=\"1\" stop-color=\"#3b2f5e\"/></linearGradient><radialGradient id=\"beam\" cx=\"0.5\" cy=\"0\" r=\"0.7\"><stop offset=\"0\" stop-color=\"#ffe25a\" stop-opacity=\"0.55\"/><stop offset=\"1\" stop-color=\"#ffe25a\" stop-opacity=\"0\"/></radialGradient></defs><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"480\" height=\"220\" fill=\"url(#bg)\"/><polygon points=\"40,30 75,120 5,120\" fill=\"url(#beam)\"/><polygon points=\"120,30 155,120 85,120\" fill=\"url(#beam)\"/><rect x=\"15\" y=\"120\" width=\"150\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#262040\"/><rect x=\"25\" y=\"105\" width=\"20\" height=\"15\" fill=\"#e2c044\"/><rect x=\"60\" y=\"100\" width=\"18\" height=\"20\" fill=\"#ef4d6e\"/><rect x=\"95\" y=\"108\" width=\"16\" height=\"12\" fill=\"#5ac8e0\"/><circle cx=\"35\" cy=\"25\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#ffe25a\"/><circle cx=\"115\" cy=\"25\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#ffe25a\"/><g fill=\"#4a3f6e\"><rect x=\"180\" y=\"125\" width=\"290\" height=\"6\"/><rect x=\"175\" y=\"140\" width=\"300\" height=\"6\"/><rect x=\"170\" y=\"155\" width=\"310\" height=\"6\"/><rect x=\"165\" y=\"170\" width=\"320\" height=\"6\"/></g><g fill=\"#7a6db5\" opacity=\"0.85\"><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"122\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"230\" cy=\"122\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"260\" cy=\"122\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"290\" cy=\"122\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"320\" cy=\"122\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"350\" cy=\"122\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"380\" cy=\"122\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"410\" cy=\"122\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"440\" cy=\"122\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"195\" cy=\"137\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"225\" cy=\"137\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"255\" cy=\"137\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"137\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"315\" cy=\"137\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"345\" cy=\"137\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"375\" cy=\"137\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"405\" cy=\"137\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"435\" cy=\"137\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"190\" cy=\"152\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"220\" cy=\"152\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"152\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"152\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"152\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"340\" cy=\"152\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"370\" cy=\"152\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"400\" cy=\"152\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"152\" r=\"3\"/></g><rect x=\"160\" y=\"185\" width=\"320\" height=\"35\" fill=\"#5b4a36\"/><rect x=\"160\" y=\"180\" width=\"320\" height=\"8\" fill=\"#8b6f4e\"/><g font-size=\"7\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\"><rect x=\"175\" y=\"192\" width=\"50\" height=\"22\" fill=\"#ef4d6e\" rx=\"2\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"207\">PRETZEL</text><rect x=\"235\" y=\"192\" width=\"50\" height=\"22\" fill=\"#e2c044\" rx=\"2\"/><text x=\"260\" y=\"207\" fill=\"#3b2f5e\">NACHOS</text><rect x=\"295\" y=\"192\" width=\"50\" height=\"22\" fill=\"#5ac8e0\" rx=\"2\"/><text x=\"320\" y=\"207\" fill=\"#1a1530\">POPCORN</text><rect x=\"355\" y=\"192\" width=\"60\" height=\"22\" fill=\"#7c5cff\" rx=\"2\"/><text x=\"385\" y=\"207\">HOT DOG</text></g><text x=\"240\" y=\"22\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#ffe25a\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\" letter-spacing=\"2\">SOLD OUT  TONIGHT</text></svg>"
    },
    "launch": "Show the arena image and tell the story: 6,400 fans, four snacks, and one nervous concessions manager. Read the table out loud. Send groups to boards before they ask for clarification — they'll figure out the proportional reasoning by arguing about it.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Optional: calculators"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "random-sampling",
      "proportional-reasoning",
      "predicting-from-a-sample",
      "expected-value",
      "sample-bias"
    ],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Strong BTC. Low floor: anyone can compute one prediction. Diamond is Q4, where students push back on the sampling method — the math says nachos will sell out, but only if the main-entrance fans look like the whole arena. Q3 is a satisfying multi-step total.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Most groups will set up part-to-whole proportions: sample shows 24/80 want pretzels, so scale by 6400/80 = 80x. That gives 1920 pretzels.\n- Some groups will compute the snack PERCENTAGE first (24/80 = 30%) and then take 30% of 6400. Same answer, slightly different path.\n- Watch for groups who try to scale by 6400/76 (excluding 'nothing'). That's a real choice — does the promoter want predicted SALES (use 6400 * 24/80) or predicted RATIO AMONG BUYERS? Surface this in the consolidation.\n- Q3 invites either: profit-per-item summed, OR (revenue total) - (cost total). Both should agree.\n- Q4 should produce real talk about whether main-entrance fans buy differently than other-entrance fans. Strong groups suggest stratifying across all four entrances.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Forgetting to scale the sample at all and reporting sample counts as final answers (24 pretzels for the whole arena).\n- Treating 'Nothing' as a snack to sell — predicting 320 'nothings' as a line item.\n- Computing revenue (price × count) and reporting that as profit, ignoring the cost-to-make column.\n- Confusing the sampling method: students may call this a simple random sample. It is actually SYSTEMATIC (every 25th ticket-holder) and the worker only sampled ONE of four entrances, which makes it a convenience-flavored systematic sample.",
    "discussionQuestions": "- Two groups predicted different nacho counts. Where does the disagreement come from — the math or the assumption?\n- Profit and revenue are not the same. How did you make sure your final number was profit?\n- The promoter could have surveyed 800 fans instead of 80. What would that have bought him? Could a larger sample still be biased?",
    "extensions": "- The 'nothing' fans bought nothing AT CONCESSIONS, but the promoter notices many of them brought refillable water bottles from outside. He decides to add a $4 reusable cup deal. Predict how many cups he should stock if 30% of the 'nothing' fans would switch to the cup deal. How does that affect total profit?\n- Suppose, after the show, actual sales were: 1,800 pretzels, 1,650 nachos, 1,720 popcorn, 1,000 hot dogs. For each snack, find the percent error of the prediction. Which prediction was the closest? Which was the worst, and what could explain it?\n- Re-design the sampling plan. The promoter wants to estimate within ±5% of the true population proportion. Without computing a formal margin of error, write a sampling plan that you'd trust more — say where, how, and how many.",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. Scale factor = 6400 / 80 = 80. Predictions:\n  - Pretzel: 24 × 80 = 1,920\n  - Nachos: 18 × 80 = 1,440\n  - Popcorn: 22 × 80 = 1,760\n  - Hot dog: 12 × 80 = 960\n  - Nothing: 4 × 80 = 320 (will not buy)\n  Buyers + non-buyers sum to 6,400. Good check.\n2. Predicted nachos = 1,440. Station can prep 1,600. NO, they will not run out — they have 160 nachos of slack (about 11%).\n3. Profit per item × predicted count:\n  - Pretzel: (6.00 − 1.25) × 1920 = 4.75 × 1920 = $9,120\n  - Nachos: (8.50 − 2.40) × 1440 = 6.10 × 1440 = $8,784\n  - Popcorn: (5.50 − 0.80) × 1760 = 4.70 × 1760 = $8,272\n  - Hot dog: (7.00 − 2.10) × 960 = 4.90 × 960 = $4,704\n  - Total predicted profit ≈ $30,880\n4. Sampling only the main entrance is a weakness. Fans at different entrances may differ — VIP, family section, student section, etc. A better plan would be to sample every 25th fan at EACH entrance proportional to entrance traffic, i.e. a stratified random sample.",
